  "id": "mbende_jerusarema",
  "name": "Mbende Jerusarema (Zezuru dance-drumming)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Dance of the Zezuru Shona of Murewa and Uzumba-Maramba-Pfungwe in Mashonaland East, originally called mbende ('mole') and renamed Jerusarema in the colonial period to make it acceptable to missionaries. Men and women dance to drumming, singing and hand-clapping, the men in crouching, acrobatic movements; UNESCO has proclaimed it a Masterpiece of the Oral and Intangible Heritage of Humanity. The generic membrane-drum card stands in for the Shona ngoma drums.",
  "recipe": "classic modern mbende jerusarema, groove Percussion, central east south African branch. membrane drum pulse resonant acoustic membrane drum, hand clap. field recording. ORTF stereo pair, portable field-recorder pre. 24-bit digital.",
